    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d752">

                <pb facs="0557" n="553" xml:id="pg553"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">494.11/90: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-12-10T18:00:00-05:00">December 10, 1938—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>420. Reference Department’s 64, February 18, 6 p.m. So far as Department’s
                    records show numerous Embassy notes regarding bombing by Japanese planes of
                    American mission property in China addressed to Foreign Office in pursuance of
                    telegram under reference remain unanswered. See for example following numbered
                    notes sent during month of May: 923, 925,<anchor n="58" xml:id="d752-f1"/> 926,
                    927, 935, 944, 946<anchor n="59" xml:id="d752-f2"/> and 947.<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d752-f3"/></p>
                <p>Unless you perceive some objection thereto, it is suggested that, subject to
                    appropriate check of Embassy records, you address a note to the Foreign Office
                    in which, after referring to (1) the above unanswered notes, (2) your note 975
                    of June 28,<anchor n="61" xml:id="d752-f4"/> which remains unanswered despite
                    Vice Minister’s promise of immediate investigation (see your 560 August 26, 8
                    p.m. to Department), (3) your unanswered note 942 May 30<anchor n="58"
                        xml:id="d752-f5"/> regarding Nantungchow bombing, sent in pursuance of
                    Foreign Office’s request, (4) your unanswered note 1105 of October 31<anchor
                        n="62" xml:id="d752-f6"/> regarding bombing of mission at Tungpeh, resulting
                    in destruction of American property and death and injury of American citizens,
                    you state that your Government desires to be informed whether it may expect a
                    formal expression of regret on the part of the Japanese Government for death and
                    injury of American citizens in Tungpeh bombing and also assurances that
                    appropriate indemnities will be granted, and when it may expect replies to the
                    other above-mentioned notes, and notes subsequently sent, regarding repeated
                    bombings of American mission properties, including schools, churches, and
                    hospitals by Japanese planes.<anchor n="63" xml:id="d752-f7"/></p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
